  <article>
    <h1>Content-addressed build caching at Fernwood Systems</h1>
    <p class="byline">By Dana Okonkwo · 12 March 2026</p>

    <p>Fernwood Systems is a 40-person infrastructure company based in Halifax,
    Nova Scotia. In January 2026 we replaced our timestamp-based build cache
    with a content-addressed one, and the change turned out to be less about
    storage and more about honesty.</p>

    <h2>What changed</h2>

    <p>The old cache keyed each artifact on the modification time of the files
    that produced it. The new cache keys every artifact on the SHA-256 hash of
    its declared inputs, so two builds with identical inputs are guaranteed to
    resolve to the same cache entry regardless of when they ran.</p>

    <p>Median CI build time on our main branch fell from 14 minutes to 3 minutes
    20 seconds after the migration. The cache hit rate on that branch now sits
    at 82 percent, measured over the eight weeks since the cutover.</p>

    <h2>What it cost</h2>

    <p>The migration took six weeks of one engineer's time and required
    rewriting 41 build files to declare their inputs explicitly. Roughly a third
    of those files were reading configuration from the environment without
    declaring it — the single most common source of cache misses we found.</p>

    <p>Storage costs rose from $310 to $1,900 per month, because we retain
    evicted cache entries for 30 days so that a bisect across a release window
    still hits warm entries.</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>The hardest part was not the hashing. It was teaching the build to be
      honest about its inputs.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>What we did not do</h2>

    <p>We evaluated moving to a remote execution cluster at the same time and
    decided against it for now: the cache alone recovered most of the wall-clock
    win, and remote execution would have added a second failure domain to every
    build. We expect to revisit that decision once the cache hit rate stops
    improving.</p>

    <p>The rollout to the other three engineering teams is planned for the third
    quarter of 2026.</p>
  </article>

